itutionally bernard smith al  jazeera  let's get an update from our producer  garam vardanyan who's joining us on the  phone from yerevan just tell us what  you're seeing around you and what's  happening on the ground garam  hello the pashinya's rally just finished  uh he was only one who was speaking  during this rally and now people are  marching in the  streets of yerevan his supporters  are marching  [Music]  he is leaving the mark uh  with the heavy presence of the police  security guys  but he is leading the march people  [Music]  march is the opposite of the  second square mission as you know  opposition had another rally  in the another square but this  yes rally uh moving not towards the  second square but opposite side of the  square so they are marching  during his speech uh he was saying that  all the together  [Music]  that we will have to show uh  our will to stay at the power because if  they  i was elected uh and you should decide  what will happen with you what can you  tell us about the presence of any  security  uh garam and what is the mood like  amongst the people and those protesters  on the streets right now  um so i i think  two different moves today like  in the world where uh i've seen uh  opposition  uh developing uh resolution of  pakistan's industry  and in the second square right now i can  see people  who are supporting pakistan  we are not want the other to come back  to the power  the previous uh authority uh  so this is the first  rally  [Music]  is you say we do you want  me to start negotiations or consultation  for past elections people are chanting  no we need no  uh elections so possibly  not thinking right now about  election even how much support would you  say  passion yan has right now in the country  i hear very very clearly  i'm asking about the support that the  prime minister has in the country  uh his main support are the people he  like his main support is  [Applause]  in the parliament and he always rely on  the people  and he only supports people  his only support are people in industry  so  i think why he was  doing this rally right now he's doing  this valley to steal  the power of the people and to feel how  much  support he has from the people  what about what the military is doing  right now because we know  that at least 40 high-ranking military  officials have called  for passion yan's resignation garam so  how significant would be the next steps  for the military  uh we are here we have some development  uh we know  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  okay we'll leave it there a little  difficult to hear you right now  but thank you so much for that update uh  garam from the right in the center of  yerevan  garam reporting on the prime minister  marching with his supporters and his  protesters in the capital  uh you can see in the live picture right  now on our screen  uh let's uh speak to robin foresee  walker robin is joining us now  from so so rob and i'm not sure if you  you were able to hear what our producer  had to say but certainly the life  picture speaks  for itself and uh the prime minister is  out right now on the streets with his  with his supporters  and and the protesters sounds like it  could be a pretty  tense situation what do you think is  going to happen next  you know giggum was talking then about  the fact that  uh mr pachinyan has been trying well has  has signed the the the dismissal of  the head of the military um but the  problem  he now faces is a constitutional one  because the president  has uh so far not put his signature on  that dismissal  um so directly in uh in in  contradicting mr pacinian which seems as  if  from the political side he is running  out of  allies with the president  uh presumably uh reluctant to go ahead  with his orders  and the military reiterating uh their  decision to re call him to on him to  resign  um and also interestingly enough stating  that their their  letter or their statement was not made  without any with with any out  uh side interference i think that's a  nod to the suggestion that maybe  russia was somehow uh involved or  or may have been putting pressure or  finding a way to  to put pressure on mr pacinian through  their  contacts in the military um so  yes it's a very fragile  uh delicate situation for passion  at the same time we have seen very large  numbers  it appears coming out onto the streets  of the capital to republic square to  show their support for him  and uh and and that means that there is  still a lot  more to play for the question is whether  we will see the military  move uh physically into the capital  mr pacinian has called on on the  military on  on the troops to continue to protect the  borders  and called for calm so a lot to still  can still happen in  in a few short hours uh but  he's always uh resorted to relying on  the public because the armenian people  have shown time and again that they  still support this idea that mr pacinian  brought to them which is that they had  the power to make changes in the country  and those were the changes that they  wanted to see which were important ones  political and economic reforms that that  they were hoping would bring an end  to the corruption and the mismanagement  that we saw in armenia prior  to mr pacquiao coming to power but the  karabakh  war last year has really changed  everything given how disastrous it was  for armenia  the the humiliation the loss of of all  of those  of those territories that they held and  the the huge  human toll the thousands of soldiers the  military  personnel who died in that war more than  three and a half thousand  armenian personnel and all of those  families armenia is a small country and  everyone knows  someone who lost a brother or a husband  in that conflict in that fighting yeah  robin and the prime minister has also  said that  change of power can only happen via  early or regular election  so is that the direction armenia is  heading in how likel